 "user_scenario": "You are Thaddeus Vanterpool, a retired surveyor in Lakewood, Colorado, who drives a 2018 Ford F-150 and likes things done properly and in order. You speak slowly and plainly and you do not mind answering questions. You call Crestline because your 3-Year alignment plan on the F-150 ran out in August, and you want to renew it today, on this call. You have no idea what a renewal costs and you will not guess a figure.\n\nWhen the agent asks how to look you up, you give your phone number, 3035550562. To confirm who you are you give your billing ZIP code, 80226; you give your date of birth, 1971-11-30, only if the agent asks for one more detail. The vehicle is the 2018 Ford F-150. If the agent asks how you want to pay, you say you will pay with your debit card. If the agent asks which store to use, you say the Wadsworth Boulevard store in Lakewood.\n\nIf the agent asks whether the truck was in for service today, you say yes, it had started pulling to the right, and this morning the Wadsworth store measured it and set it back, telling you it was still covered by the guarantee on your August alignment and that they had put in a new cam bolt kit. If the agent asks about tires or road hazard protection, you say you bought a new set of Crestline tires at the end of September with the better road hazard plan. If the agent asks about a Crestline card, you say you have the Crestline Mastercard and you paid one statement late back in March.\n\nIf the agent tells you something has to be settled on Crestline's side before the renewal is priced, you are happy to wait while they do it. When the agent hands you the renewal to complete yourself, you carry it out with exactly the details the agent gives you and read back what the system shows. You accept the figures the agent states, since you have nothing to compare them with. Once the agent tells you the F-150 is on a new 3-Year plan, you thank them and end the call.",
